it, even when they feel\nlike a single natural unit. "It feels like one word" is not a reason to keep it.\n\nDo not over-decompose: stop when a part is not itself a freestanding word\n\nException — keep the compound as its own card (alongside any reusable roots) ONLY\nwhen a learner who already knows every root still could not arrive at the meaning,\nbecause it's idiomatic or misleading:\n  - Kindergarten — not a "children's garden"\n  - Handschuh — "hand-shoe" = glove\nTransparency test: would knowing the parts let the learner derive the meaning?\nYes → split, no compound card. Reach for this exception rarely.\n\nWorked example:\nInput: "Ich trinke jeden Morgen Apfelsaft."\n  - vocabulary: "Apfel"  (reusable root)\n  - vocabulary: "Saft"   (reusable root)\n  - NO "Apfelsaft" card — apple + juice is fully transparent\n\nOTHER PRODUCTION RULES\nOther functions with competing forms deserve the same per-function production\ncards: pronoun choice (tag production-which-pronoun), Perfekt vs Präteritum,\nwissen vs kennen, wenn/wann/als/ob, and similar.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n(Preposition cards shown; other cards like verbs/nouns also apply.)\n\nInput: "Am Wochenende fahren wir in die Berge."\nPrepositions present: an (am), in.\n  - vocabulary: "an"   tags: ["preposition"]\n  - vocabulary: "in"   tags: ["preposition"]\n  - grammar (production-which-preposition):\n      "Choosing 'when': an (days/parts of week) / in (months, seasons, periods)\n       / um (clock times)"\n       → am Wochenende uses an because it's a part of the week\n  - grammar (production-which-preposition):\n      "Choosing 'to a place': nach/zu/in/auf/an"\n       → in die Berge uses in: an area you move into, takes an article\n\nInput: "Im Sommer fliege ich zu meiner Schwester."\nPrepositions present: in (im), zu.\n  - vocabulary: "in"   tags: ["preposition"]\n  - vocabulary: "zu"   tags: ["preposition"]\n  - grammar (production-which-preposition):\n      "Choosing 'when': an (days/parts of week) / in (mont